<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-01">
  <title>Week 1</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This is an outline of the topics we covered in the first week of class.
    </p>
  </introduction>


  <subsection>
    <title>Tuesday 1/13</title>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-Introduction">
      <title>Ch 0: Introduction</title>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            A population changes over time.
            Let <m>P(t)</m> be the population at time <m>t</m>.
            Each year, some proportion (<m>k_{\text{birth}}</m>) produce offspring and some proportion (<m>k_{\text{death}}</m>) dies.
            So:
            <md>
              <mrow> \frac{dP}{dt} = k_{\text{birth}} P - k_{\text{death}} P = kP, </mrow>
            </md>
            where <m>k = k_{\text{birth}} - k_{\text{death}}</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>

      <p>
        The equation <m>P' = kP</m> is called a <term>differential equation (DE)</term>, an equation which relates a function to one or more of its own derivatives.
      </p>

      <definition xml:id="def-DE-solution">
        <statement>
          <p>
            A <term>solution</term> to a DE is a function that satisfies the DE.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Given <m>P' = kP</m>, let <m>P(t) = e^{kt}</m>.
            Then <m>P'(t) = ke^{kt} = k P(t)</m>, so this <m>P(t)</m> is a solution to the DE.
          </p>

          <p>
            Are there others? Can we find another solution? Can we find all solutions? Consider the following calculation:
            <md>
              <mrow> \frac{dP}{dt} \amp = kP </mrow>
              <mrow> \frac{1}{P}\ dP \amp = k\ dt </mrow>
              <mrow> \int \frac{1}{P}\ dP \amp = \int k\ dt </mrow>
              <mrow> \ln|P| \amp = kt + C </mrow>
              <mrow> |P| \amp = e^{kt + C} = e^Ce^{kt} = Ce^{kt}. </mrow>
            </md>
            So <m>P = Ce^{kt}</m> is the <term>general solution</term> to the DE.
          </p>

          <p>
            Notice the sloppiness with the constant of integration <m>C</m> above.
            This sort of thing will be typical.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>

      <p>
        So solutions to a DE are not unique.
        However, if we specify <term>initial conditions (IC)</term>, we can identify a particular solution of interest.
        The IC takes the form of a single point of our unknown function.
      </p>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Suppose <m>P' = 1.1 P</m> and <m>P(0) = 100</m>.
            We know that <m>P(t) = Ce^{1.1 t}</m> is the general solution to the DE.
            Plugging in the IC:
            <md>
              <mrow> 100 = Ce^{1.1(0)} = C, </mrow>
            </md>
            so <m>P(t) = 100 e^{1.1t}</m> is the particular population function we're interested in.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>

      <p>
        A DE together with an IC is called an <term>initial value problem (IVP)</term>.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-Slope-Fields">
      <title>Sec 1.2: Slope Fields</title>

      <p>
        A <term>1st order DE</term> has the form <m>y' = f(x, y)</m>, where <m>f(x, y)</m> is some expression in terms of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.
        In this case, <m>y'</m> gives the slope of the tangent line to the graph of <m>y(x)</m> at any point in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
      </p>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Suppose an object falls subject to gravity and air resistance (which we'll assume is proportional to velocity).
            Then:
            <md>
              <mrow> \frac{dv}{dt} = g - \frac{\gamma v}{m}, </mrow>
            </md>
            where <m>g</m> is the gravitational constant, <m>\gamma</m> is the drag coefficient, and <m>m</m> is the mass.
            For a more specific example, suppose <m>\frac{dv}{dt} = 9.8 - \frac{v}{5}</m>.
            Then, at various points in the <m>tv</m>-plane, we know that a solution function <m>v(t)</m> passing through that point must do so with slope <m>9.8 - \frac{v}{5}</m>.
            We can draw many sample slopes to get a <term>slope field</term>, which gives a sense of the shape of <term>solution curves</term> (aka <term>integral curves</term>):
          </p>

          <figure xml:id="fig-slope-field">
            <caption>Slope field for a first order DE.</caption>
            <image width="40%">
              <description>
                <p>
                  Slope field for <m>\frac{dv}{dt} = 9.8 - \frac{v}{5}</m> showing several approximate solution curves.
                </p>
              </description>
              <latex-image>
                \begin{tikzpicture}[declare function={f(\x,\y) = -0.8*\y +  1.2;},scale=0.7]
                \def\xmin{-3}
                \def\xmax{3}
                \def\ymin{-3}
                \def\ymax{3}
                \def\nx{12}
                \def\ny{12}
                \draw [->] ({\xmin - 0.5}, 0) to ({\xmax + 0.5}, 0) node [right] {$t$};
                \draw [->] (0, {\ymin - 0.5}) to (0, {\ymax + 0.5}) node [above] {$y$};
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\hx}{(\xmax-\xmin)/\nx}
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\hy}{(\ymax-\ymin)/\ny}
                \foreach \i in {0,...,\nx}
                \foreach \j in {0,...,\ny}{
                  \pgfmathsetmacro{\yprime}{f({\xmin+\i*\hx},{\ymin+\j*\hy})}
                  \draw[black,shift={({\xmin+\i*\hx},{\ymin+\j*\hy})}] 
                  (0,0)--($(0,0)!3mm! (.2,.2*\yprime)$);
                }
                \draw [very thick,blue,domain=-2:3.5,samples=50] plot (\x, {1.5 - 0.8 * exp(-0.8 * \x)});
                \draw [very thick,blue,domain=0.3:3.5,samples=50] plot (\x, {1.5 + 2 * exp(-0.8 * \x)});
                \draw [very thick,blue] (-3, 1.5) to (3.5, 1.5);
                \end{tikzpicture}
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
        </statement>
      </example>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-Picard">
        <title>Picard's Theorem</title>

        <statement>
          <p>
            Given an IVP <m>y' = f(x, y), y(x_0) = y_0</m>, if <m>f</m> is continuous and <m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m> exists and is continuous near <m>(x_0, y_0)</m>, then a solution to the IVP exists and is unique in an interval.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </theorem>

      <p>
        The symbol <m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m> is called a <term>partial derivative</term>.
        Since Calc III is not prerequisite to this course, I'll just say that, for our purposes, the idea of partial derivatives is: when a function has multiple variables, partial derivatives treat all but one variable as constant.
        For example, if <m>f(x, y) = xy + y^2</m>, then:
        <md>
          <mrow> \frac{\partial f}{\partial x} \amp = y \amp \frac{\partial f}{\partial y} \amp = x + 2y </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Thursday 1/15</title>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-EU">
      <title>Existence and Uniqueness</title>

      <p>
        Recall <xref ref="thm-Picard"/> from last time.
        Let's see an example.
      </p>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Consider <m>y' = xy^{3/2} = f(x, y)</m>.
            Then <m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial y} = \frac{3}{2} xy^{1/2}</m>.
            Both <m>f, \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m> are continuous when <m>y \geq 0</m>.
          </p>

          <figure xml:id="fig-Picard">
            <caption>Three possible initial condition points.</caption>
            <image width="50%">
              <description>
                <p>
                  In the <m>xy</m>-plane, the upper half plane is shaded.
                  A point labeled <m>a</m> is in the upper half plane, with strictly positive <m>y</m>-value.
                  A point labeled <m>b</m> lies on the <m>x</m>-axis.
                  A point labeled <m>c</m> is in the unshaded region below the <m>x</m>-axis.
                </p>
              </description>
              <latex-image>
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                \fill [pattern=dots] (-3, 0) rectangle (3, 3);
                \draw [->] (-3, 0) to (3, 0) node [right] {$x$};
                \draw [->] (0, -1) to (0, 3) node [above] {$y$};
                \node [circle, fill, inner sep=2pt, label=below:$a$] at (2, 2) {};
                \node [circle, fill, inner sep=2pt, label=below:$b$] at (1, 0) {};
                \node [circle, fill, inner sep=2pt, label=below:$c$] at (-1, -0.5) {};
                \draw [dashed] (2, 2) circle (0.5);
                \draw [dashed] (1, 0) circle (0.5);
                \end{tikzpicture}
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>

          <p>
            The theorem clearly does not apply at the initial condition point <m>c</m>.
            At this point, <m>y \\lt 0</m>, so the functions <m>f, \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m> are not defined.
          </p>

          <p>
            At <m>a</m>, <m>y > 0</m>, so both <m>f, \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m> exist.
            More importantly, we can draw a small circle around that point, and <m>f, \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m> are continuous in the entire circle (aka, "near" <m>a</m>).
            So the theorem applies at <m>a</m>.
          </p>

          <p>
            At <m>b</m>, both <m>f, \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m> exist.
            However, <em>any</em> circle around <m>b</m> includes points where <m>f, \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m> don't exist.
            So the theorem doesn't apply at <m>b</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>

      <p>
        Notation: for brevity, we can write <m>f_y = \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m>.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-Separable-Equations">
      <title>Separable Equations</title>

      <definition xml:id="def-separable">
        <statement>
          <p>
            A DE is <term>separable</term> if it can be written in the form:
            <md>
              <mrow>\frac{dy}{dx} = y' = f(x)g(y) </mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <p>
        In this case:
        <md>
          <mrow> h(y)\ dy = \frac{1}{g(y)}\ dy \amp = f(x)\ dx </mrow>
          <mrow> \Rightarrow \quad \int h(y)\ dy \amp = \int f(x)\ dx </mrow>
        </md>
        Question: Is it okay to separate the differentials like this? To integrate each side?
      </p>

      <p>
        Justification:
        <md>
          <mrow> \frac{dy}{dx} \amp = f(x) \frac{1}{h(y)} </mrow>
          <mrow> h(y) \frac{dy}{dx} \amp = f(x) </mrow>
          <mrow> \int h(y) \frac{dy}{dx}\ dx \amp = \int f(x)\ dx  </mrow>
          <mrow> \int h(u)\ du \amp = \int f(x)\ dx </mrow>
          <mrow> \int h(y)\ dy \amp = \int f(x)\ dx </mrow>
        </md>
        (Note the <m>u</m>-substitution <m>u = y</m> moving from line 3 to line 4.)
      </p>

      <p>
        We don't need to reshow this justification every time we separate variables.
        But now we see that it's not shenanigans or a magic trick, it's just the Chain Rule.
      </p>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Solve <m>y' = \frac{x}{y}</m>.
            <md>
              <mrow>  \int y\ dy \amp = \int x\ dx </mrow>
              <mrow> \frac{y^2}{2} \amp \frac{x^2}{2} + C </mrow>
              <mrow> y^2 \amp = x^2 + C. </mrow>
            </md>
            This is an <term>implicit solution</term> to the DE.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Solve <m>\frac{dy}{dx} = y^2 - 2xy^2 = f(x, y)</m>.
            <md>
              <mrow> \frac{dy}{dx} \amp (1 - 2x)y^2 </mrow>
              <mrow> \int y^{-2}\ dy \amp = \int 1 - 2x\ dx </mrow>
              <mrow> \frac{-1}{y} \amp = x - x^2 + C </mrow>
              <mrow> y \amp = \frac{1}{x^2 - x + C} </mrow>
            </md>
            However, moving from line 2 to line 3, we divided by <m>y^2</m>.
            This assumed that <m>y \neq 0</m>.
            Looking back at the DE, it seems that <m>y = 0</m> <em>is</em> a solution.
            So we must remember to include it when we write the general solution.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-First-Order-Linear">
      <title>First Order Linear</title>

      <definition xml:id="def-First-Order-Linear">
        <statement>
          <p>
            A DE which can be put in the form
            <md>
              <mrow> y' + p(x) y \amp f(x) </mrow>
            </md>
            is called a <term>first order linear equation</term>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <p>
        Idea: to solve these DEs, we notice that the LHS almost looks like a derivative taken via the Product Rule.
        Can we find a factor function <m>r(x)</m> to multiply both sides of the DE to get:
        <md>
          <mrow> ry' + rpy \amp = ry' + r' y? </mrow>
        </md>
        If so, then:
        <md>
          <mrow> ry' + rpy \amp = ry' + r' y </mrow>
          <mrow> rpy \amp = r' y </mrow>
          <mrow> rp \amp = r' </mrow>
          <mrow> \int \frac{r'(x)}{r(x)}\ dx \amp = \int p(x)\ dx </mrow>
          <mrow> \ln|r(x)| \amp = \int p(x)\ dx </mrow>
          <mrow> r(x) \amp = e^{\int p(x)\ dx} </mrow>
        </md>
        This function is called an <term>integrating factor</term>.
      </p>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <md>
              <mrow> v' \amp = 9.8 - \frac{v}{5} </mrow>
              <mrow> v' + \frac{v}{5} \amp = 9.8 </mrow>
              <mrow> r(t) \amp = e^{\int \frac{1}{5}\ dt} = e^{t/5} </mrow>
              <mrow> \Rightarrow e^{t/5} v' + \frac{e^{t/5}}{5} v \amp = 9.8 e^{t/5} </mrow>
              <mrow> \int \left(e^{t/5} v\right)'\ dt \amp = \int 9.8 e^{t/5}\ dt </mrow>
              <mrow> e^{t/5} v  \amp = 49 e^{t/5} + C </mrow>
              <mrow> v  \amp = 49 + C e^{-t/5} </mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Solve <m>y' = 2x(2 - y), y(0) = 5</m>.
            <md>
              <mrow> y' \amp 4x - 2xy </mrow>
              <mrow> y' + 2xy  \amp = 4x </mrow>
              <mrow> r(x) \amp = e^{\int p(x)\ dx} = e^{\int 2x\ dx} = e^{x^2} </mrow>
              <mrow> \int \left(e^{x^2} y\right)'\ dx \amp = \int 4xe^{x^2}\ dx </mrow>
              <mrow> e^{x^2} y \amp = 2e^{x^2} + C </mrow>
              <mrow> y \amp = 2 + Ce^{-x^2} </mrow>
            </md>
            Applying the IC <m>y(0) = 5</m>:
            <md>
              <mrow> 5 \amp = 2 + Ce^0 </mrow>
              <mrow> C \amp = 3 </mrow>
              <mrow> \Rightarrow y \amp = 2 + 3e^{-x^2} </mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>
</section>